WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.733 --> 00:00:08.500
One coding agent feels like a superpower.

2
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:10.167
You describe a feature.

3
00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:11.833
It writes some code.

4
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:12.867
You make a coffee.

5
00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:14.300
Things are looking good.

6
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.167
Then you try running five agents.

7
00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:18.467
Or twenty.

8
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:20.933
Three edit the same file.

9
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.800
Four launch the entire test suite.

10
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:26.000
Your laptop starts preparing for takeoff.

11
00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:30.300
And two agents ask you the same
question while a third confidently

12
00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.267
implements the opposite answer.

13
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:34.000
Congratulations.

14
00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.967
You've become a middle manager for robots.

15
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:49.133
That's the problem Agent
Q is built to solve.

16
00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:51.200
The name has two meanings.

17
00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:53.933
Queue, because you queue
up work for agents.

18
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.433
And Q, as in the gadget
maker from the Bond films:

19
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.933
the workshop that equips
you for the mission.

20
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:03.433
In this case, the gadgets are software.

21
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:06.400
You bring the ideas and the judgment.

22
00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:10.833
Agent Q organizes the agents,
their tools, and the work

23
00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:12.867
needed to build them.

24
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:16.600
Underneath, it's a background service
that coordinates coding agents,

25
00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:20.267
gives them separate places to work,
and carries finished branches

26
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:23.000
through checks toward the
shared development branch.

27
00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:27.400
One of the original
motivations was simple:

28
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:31.667
get more useful work out of the
Claude and Codex subscriptions you're

29
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:33.367
already paying for.

30
00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:36.800
Whether that's one twenty-dollar
plan or several two-hundred-dollar

31
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.700
plans, the aim is to make good
use of the capacity available to

32
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:41.467
you.

33
00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:43.000
Keep a backlog ready.

34
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.433
Assign suitable work.

35
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.100
Watch reported usage and
provider availability.

36
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:51.700
Let independent tasks move while
something else is waiting.

37
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.467
The goal is useful, checked software
for the capacity you've paid

38
00:01:56.667 --> 00:01:57.033
for.

39
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.200
Otherwise, we've invented a very
elaborate way to turn tokens into

40
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.033
laptop heat.

